   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the insurance law, in relation to indexing fixed amounts and\nclarifying compliance for certain life insurance and annuity businesses; and\nto repeal certain provisions of such law relating to obsolete language and\nunnecessary filing requirements",
    "basePrintNo": "S10017",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S10017-2025",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 23,
       "fullName": "Jessica Scarcella-Spanton",
       "imgName": "1329_Jessica_Scarcella-Spanton.jpg",
       "incumbent": true,
       "memberId": 1512,
       "sessionMemberId": 2340,
       "sessionYear": 2025,
       "shortName": "SCARCELLA-SPANTON"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §4228, rpld §4228 sub§ (g), sub§ (f) ¶1 sub¶ (A), Ins L",
    "lawSection": "Insurance Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S10017\n\nSPONSOR: BAILEY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the insurance law, in relation to indexing fixed amounts\nand clarifying compliance for certain life insurance and annuity busi-\nnesses; and to repeal certain provisions of such law relating to obso-\nlete language and unnecessary filing requirements\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThis bill would modernize Insurance Law Section 4228 by indexing fixed\ndollar amounts in the statute to inflation, authorizing changes to\ncompensation plans, removing obsolete language, and relieving insurers\nof unnecessary filing requirements.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 amends Insurance Law Section 4228 to index training allowance\nsubsidy limits to inflation.\n\nSection 2 amends Insurance Law Section 4228 to index prize and award\nlimits to inflation.\n\nSection 3 amends Insurance Law Section 4228 to permit changes to\nin-force compensation.\n\nSection 4 amends Insurance Law Section 4228 to repeal subsection (g) as\na technical cleanup, because that subsection applied only during the\nfirst four years following the 1998 enactment of Section 4228.\n\nSection 5 amends Insurance Law Section 4228 to eliminate filings under\nparagraph (f)(1)(A), thereby reducing administrative burden.\n\nSection 6 amends Insurance Law Section 4228 to revise the definition of\ntotal selling expenses.\n\nSection 7 sets forth the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThis legislation builds on important steps taken to modernize Insurance\nLaw Section 4228 in 2025. Section 4228 is significant to New York-li-\ncensed life insurers because it is extraterritorial; that is, it applies\nto policies sold outside this state involving nonresidents of this\nstate. The result, therefore, is that it limits the amount of agent\ncompensation that may be paid on sales of individual life insurance and\nannuity business regardless of where in the country that business is\nsold.\n\nNew York Insurance Law places limits on training allowance subsidies and\non prizes and awards for agents and brokers that are paid for by insur-\nance companies. Training allowance subsidies allow life insurers to\nprovide financial support to agents while they are learning the business\nand building their client base. Under the statute, those subsidies may\nbe paid for four years, after which agent compensation is based solely\non commissions.\n\nThis bill would allow those limits to be adjusted annually based on the\nsame percentage used for the state minimum wage. Over time, the statuto-\nry limits have become outdated and no longer serve their intended\npurpose because they have lost approximately half their value due to\ninflation. The Department of Financial Services has increased the limits\nonly three times in the past 17 years, one of which resulted from legis-\nlation enacted in 2025. This bill would resolve that issue on a perma-\nnent basis and provide new agents and insurers with greater certainty\ngoing forward.\n\nIn addition, this bill would allow insurance companies to make changes\nto in-force compensation plans. There is a strong public policy basis\nfor permitting increased agent compensation on older policies that were\noriginally filed, years ago, without a renewal commission.  Encouraging\nimproved agent servicing of older policies is in the best interests of\nexisting policyholders and helps ensure that their insurance needs\ncontinue to be met. The proposed amendment would specifically authorize\nan insurer to provide an agent with a renewal commission and would\nrequire the insurer to file the amended compensation plan with the\nDepartment in accordance with the same requirements that apply to a\nnewly filed product.\n\nThis bill also modernizes the Insurance Law by eliminating subsection\n(g), which applied only during the four years following its enactment in\n1998. In addition, the bill eliminates one of the many filings currently\nrequired by Section 4228 because that filing provides little, if any,\nvalue in comparison with the effort required to produce it.\n\nFinally, in addition to several definitional changes made in 2025, this\nbill further clarifies what constitutes total selling expenses.  Remov-\ning language such as \"including but not limited to\" reduces the number\nof subjective determinations insurers must make (which supports compli-\nance) and ensures consistent application among insurers. The bill also\nspecifically excludes from total selling expenses any annual marketing\nor distribution fees on a mutual fund, or any portion thereof, received\nby the insurer pursuant to Rule 12b-1 under the Investment Company Act\nof 1940 (17 C.F.R.  Sec. 270.12b-1), which is then paid by the insurer\nto the agent or broker. This language is intended to correct a long-\nstanding misinterpretation of the law: 12b-1 fees are not paid by the\ninsurance company and are not controlled by the insurance company, so\nthey should not be classified as selling expenses of the insurance\ncompany.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone to the State.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis bill shall take effect immediately\n",
